\documentclass{report}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\title{Fatigue Life of Welded Joints under Variable-Amplitude Loading}
\author{K. Lindqvist}
\date{2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\tableofcontents

\chapter{Experiments}

\section{Specimen preparation}
Forty-eight cruciform specimens were cut from the same eight-millimetre
steel plate and welded by two operators following the same procedure
qualification record. Toe dressing was applied to half of the batch,
so that the effect of post-weld treatment could be separated from the
scatter between operators. All specimens were measured after welding,
and any specimen outside the geometric tolerance was discarded before
testing rather than after, to keep the censoring scheme clean.

\section{Test programme}
Each specimen was cycled under a variable-amplitude spectrum derived
from strain-gauge recordings on the bridge deck. The spectrum was
scaled to three target stress ranges, with sixteen specimens per level.
Failure was defined as a through-thickness crack detected by the
compliance gauge, and runout was declared at ten million cycles.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}
The dressed specimens outlasted the as-welded ones at every stress
range, with the median life ratio between the two groups close to two.
Scatter within each group was modest once the two early outliers were
traceable to a misaligned clamp. The fitted stress-life curve for the
as-welded group is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sn}
N = 2.1 \times 10^{12} \, \left( \frac{\Delta \sigma}{\mathrm{MPa}} \right)^{-3.0}
\end{equation}
with the exponent fixed at the code value and only the prefactor
estimated from the data.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:results}
The measured exponent of $-3.0$ sits on the shallow end of published
welded-detail curves, which supports the decision to fix rather than
estimate it. The dominant uncertainty is the spectrum itself: small
changes in the rainflow counting shifted the predicted life by more
than the dressing did, so field spectra should be re-recorded before
the detail class is revisited.

As Section~\ref{sec:results} reported, the runout level was reached
by six of the sixteen dressed specimens at the lowest stress range,
which limits how far the fitted curve can be extrapolated.

\chapter{Conclusions}
Toe dressing roughly doubles the median fatigue life of the cruciform
detail under the bridge-deck spectrum. The improvement is consistent
across operators and stress ranges, and the remaining scatter is
dominated by spectrum uncertainty rather than by workmanship.

\end{document}

